{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia","id":1449274,"title":"Attaining Titles in Concordia?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Changeling: The Dreaming"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-22T12:20:07-07:00","id":1449274,"title":"Attaining Titles in Concordia?","content":"If one wanted to tell a story around a motley trying to become Counts or Barons, there doesn\u2019t seem to be that much to work with. Are there perhaps large segments of land that seems lost to Banality that you can claim as your own if you can establish your own Freehold and demonstrate that you have made it habitable to Changelings and Chimera? There needs to be more rules on winning Titles and what you get from those Titles. It is one thing to call yourself a Duke, it is another to be able command all changelings to evacuate a segment of your Duchy or to claim taxes and tribute from the Commoners. Is it just me or do Titles feel more like a nebulous plot device more than anything?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T13:08:36-07:00","id":1449278,"title":"","content":"Remember that these are Changelings. Ask yourself how do people become Nobles in a fairy tale. Then add a touch of cynicism. I'd read some James Thurber. &quot;The White Deer,&quot; &quot;The Thirteen Clocks,&quot; and &quot;Many Moons.&quot; The first especially blends exquisite beauty and cynical humor seamlessly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T14:07:49-07:00","id":1449282,"title":"","content":"I can't answer for Changeling, but historically commoners did not become nobles by &quot;claiming&quot; lands and setting themselves up as one. They were granted title by the sovereign. The sovereign might do so for various reasons. It might be recognition of merit or service, it might be because the receiver paid the sovereign for it (this sometimes happened when the king was in desperate need of cash), or possibly through some kind of force or trickery that left the sovereign no choice. In some cases holding certain offices (which constitutes a kind of service) automatically granted nobility. Another possibility is for a non-noble descendant to end up inheriting the nobility if no other heirs could be found.<br /> <br /> (The only times I know when commoners could rise up in ranks by themselves is during extensive civil war type scenarios when great military commanders could set themselves up as warlords and become de facto nobles recognized by others. But even here they usually needed some sort of legitimizing by a recognized sovereign. This happened in Japan during the Sengoku Jidai era.)<br /> <br /> The awarding of titles is distinct from possessing of lands. Although some titles may have lands associated with it, those tend to be titles that are part of the royal domain and stay with them, as opposed to being alienated. They're just temporarily held by the sovereign's children until they become king or die and then return to the crown. Although possibly some offices might have a very small domain that stays with the title and also cannot be alienated, as it is designed to provide the current title holder with a source of income so he can perform his duties; and when someone else is given that position, the land is given over to them. In these cases the noble should be seen as having usufruct privileges of the land rather than patrimony (the ability to use versus ownership). Though it is always possible the sovereign might choose to award actual land ownership separately from the any title in order to provide a means of living for the new noble, Most likely, the new noble's lands were just the lands they were able to legally own before then.<br /> <br /> The original intention behind feudalism was that it was not a monetized economy. There were not enough coins for the economy. So in lieu of hiring someone to manage part of your realm, the king would give the lands to a loyal retainer to rule outright, who in turn had the service and products of the lands by the people who worked it. That served as taxation in a time when people didn't have cash to pay instead.<br /> <br /> Changeling is a bizarre mix of feudal reality and fiction for the purposes of the game. It includes magical principles like &quot;the land and the king are one&quot; or that someone can &quot;wield supreme executive power just 'cause some watery tart threw a sword at you&quot;. So you can pick and choose what you like in order for the game to make sense to you and your PCs.<br /> <br /> But I would start with the idea that a fae sovereign would need to bestow a title to a commoner who has performed services for their sovereign. Or perhaps they hold some kind of tournament and bestow a gift upon the winner of making them a noble if they are already a commoner. Or start with one of them being recognized a knight, and then building the story from there as they work their way up by performing great services and heroism for the king.<br /> <br /> Or perhaps in the case of a fae helping to overthrow one of the Changeling commoner republics and re-establishing a feudal system, then perhaps that fae can proclaim himself count of duke or even king and eventually have the other nobles recognize that claim and perhaps have some sort of legitimizing ceremony performed; or because the powers of the Dreaming selected that fae to receive some symbol of authority proclaiming her a noble.<br /> <br /> These are my initial thoughts on the matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T18:58:38-07:00","id":1449300,"title":"","content":"This all sounds good, but I also need some ideas on what you should get from being a Duke or Duchess besides getting invited to all of the best parties and being the one blamed for everything that is going wrong? It seems if you tax your people glamour you are literally stealing their life's blood. At least when you are a Prince in V:tM, you get an idea of what you are getting with the title.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","user":"Mercurial","date":". 05-22-2021, 10:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/986-mercurial","name":"Mercurial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar986_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T22:15:40-07:00","id":1449319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449282#post1449282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can't answer for Changeling, but historically commoners did not become nobles by &quot;claiming&quot; lands and setting themselves up as one.</div> </div> </div> </div>Viking period england disagrees. So much of Japanese History disagrees(&quot;Helpful hint, forge documents!&quot;) the basics of it would be if you want to be a count take over some land. Be useful to Duke get rewarded. This works if you want to be King, or Shogun or Pope... it really is &quot;get an army. Hold land&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-23T02:19:49-07:00","id":1449330,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449319#post1449319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Viking period england disagrees. So much of Japanese History disagrees(&quot;Helpful hint, forge documents!&quot;) the basics of it would be if you want to be a count take over some land. Be useful to Duke get rewarded. This works if you want to be King, or Shogun or Pope... it really is &quot;get an army. Hold land&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Did you even read the actual post? I acknowledged in times of brutal warfare that commoners with martial leadership skills often rose up in ranks and even mentioned by name the Sengoku Jidai period in Japanese history.<br /> <br /> As for forging documents, this is true but usually only relevant in certain eras when literate societies prize written documents, but haven't yet discovered proper forensics and philology to counteract forgers.<br /> <br /> The idea that commoners could make themselves nobles by their own merits, as opposed to convincing sovereigns to bestow it upon them for a variety of reasons, is simply minimal. In normal circumstances, commoners are not capable of &quot;get an army&quot; that is personally loyal to them. That only happens in widespread civil disturbances or foreign invasion when society and government breaks down. When that happens, peasants can even make themselves emperor. But that is in exceptional circumstances, not normal ones.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-23T02:42:29-07:00","id":1449333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449300#post1449300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This all sounds good, but I also need some ideas on what you should get from being a Duke or Duchess besides getting invited to all of the best parties and being the one blamed for everything that is going wrong?</div> </div> </div> </div>If you are meaning the differences between noble titles, it's mainly an issue of social status. The higher the title, the bigger the prestige. That also involves precedent, higher authorities need to treat the higher nobility better than they do lower nobility.<br /> <br /> It originally meant higher power as well since the highest noble titles implied lower vassals that supported them. However, in later ages enough land was alienated that not only lower titled nobility might be wealthier, but commoners might be wealthier than any noble.<br /> <br /> Now in Changeling, there are also magical principles involved that don't apply in real history. So things like &quot;ducal treasures&quot; (or &quot;royal treasures&quot; or &quot;county treasures&quot;, etc.) are strong benefits that come with the title. A ducal pennon allows the duke to claim personal fiefs and override lower noble claims to them. They have signets that allow them to sign treaties, assign lands, make proclamations and issue writs which the lower nobles don't. Among human rulers, the &quot;land&quot; doesn't obey their rule, but it does for the fae. So likely there are certain powers such figures have that exist simply because they have that title.<br /> <br /> Probably the main thing from a Changeling perspective is that a Duke should have vassals below them, and therefore they suck up, serve, or pay lip service to the Duke, just like the Dukes do the King. And Barons to the Count. Or Knights to the Baron. A Duke isn't sovereign (normally), but he still has vassals that report to him directly. How many troops can he raise? How much Glamour does he control directly? How many treasures give him powers? How much influence and loyalty has he purchased as a result of controlling these resources that he provides to others?<br /> <br /> As for Dukes being blamed when something goes wrong, that really depends on the circumstances and how powerful (in practice) the Duke is to the King. Historically, powerful nobles got away with a lot of incompetence or failure because they were too powerful for the King to alienate and anger. Usually other scapegoats were found who couldn't fight back even if the King knew the duke (or whoever) was at fault. Even kings don't want to make enemies of other powerful people, even those who theoretically report to them. It takes effort to neutralize those who are politically powerful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449300#post1449300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems if you tax your people glamour you are literally stealing their life's blood.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's any kind of taxation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mercurial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449300#post1449300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At least when you are a Prince in V:tM, you get an idea of what you are getting with the title.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, a prince is literally &quot;first among equals&quot; so you are a sovereign not really responsible to anyone else. The Camarilla (meaning the Inner Circle and their Justicar an Archon enforcers) tries to impose their authority on the Princes, but that has always been a contentious issue.<br /> <br /> But Vampire also has the Status background which affects all sorts of things even for people who aren't Prince. If you are big into Vampire, there are probably lots of things you can take from the Status background and apply to Changeling Title.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-23T23:44:13-07:00","id":1449426,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449330#post1449330\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Did you even read the actual post? I acknowledged in times of brutal warfare that commoners with martial leadership skills often rose up in ranks and even mentioned by name the Sengoku Jidai period in Japanese history.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand most people think all of Japanese history is... the sengoku Jidai. Its not. I can point out plenty of times in more peaceful periods. Also Concordia Looks like its set up to be going into its war period so...<br /> <br /> <br /> [quoteAs for forging documents, this is true but usually only relevant in certain eras when literate societies prize written documents, but haven't yet discovered proper forensics and philology to counteract forgers.[/quote]<br /> <br /> <br /> I am speicifically calling out a repeated pattern across Japanese History from the Heian Period to the Meiji Restoration, and while the Sengoku Jidai falls within that period its not the only one. But claiming descent from some noble, having the right anyway is a pretty standard <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The idea that commoners could make themselves nobles by their own merits, as opposed to convincing sovereigns to bestow it upon them for a variety of reasons, is simply minimal. In normal circumstances, commoners are not capable of &quot;get an army&quot; that is personally loyal to them. That only happens in widespread civil disturbances or foreign invasion when society and government breaks down. When that happens, peasants can even make themselves emperor. But that is in exceptional circumstances, not normal ones. </div> </div> </div>There's alot of angry commoners out there looking to take down nobles. There's alot of nobles who don't like other nobles. The Dreaming also.. randomly hands out titles. Historically how did someone become &quot;first of their line&quot; through being helpful to someone or violence. Changeling adds all sorts of mythic &quot;you are the one chosen person to guide this...&quot; and all sorts of fantasy options to the mix.<br /> <br /> Yeah most peasants didn't become Emperors... because what they did was stay at home and farm. Most Nobles didn't become Emperors. Jumping up the ranks is all about being the exception.. but.. the Dreaming values narrative more than base reality...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-24T00:43:41-07:00","id":1449433,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449426#post1449426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand most people think all of Japanese history is... the sengoku Jidai. Its not. I can point out plenty of times in more peaceful periods. Also Concordia Looks like its set up to be going into its war period so...</div> </div> </div> </div>I am so very glad my post has caused you to respond in such a condescending manner over your knowledge of Japanese history. While I am no expert, I do read and write Japanese as well has having lived and worked there. So please excuse me if I do not quiver from your superior knowledge of being able to name drop terms like Heian and Meiji.<br /> <br /> I specifically mentioned the Sengoku Jidai because Toyotomi Hideyoshi famously rose from being a peasant to de facto supreme ruler of Japan (and went through various means to have official nobles legitimize him). I later mentioned peasant to emperor because of Hongwu, the first Ming Emperor of China who rose from peasant to Emperor of China and founder of a new dynasty. But those are exceptions, not standard.<br /> <br /> The original poster didn't seem to have a good understanding of how commoners could become nobles. So my intent was to give him some very general historical knowledge in a relatively short post and let him know of some exceptions, and how Changeling supernatural elements could supplement that. It might not have been a perfect summary, but hey I spent ten minutes on it.<br /> <br /> If you want to add something to a thread, you can do so while not attempting to shitpost on someone else. Be more productive than that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449426#post1449426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But claiming descent from some noble, having the right anyway is a pretty standard</div> </div> </div> </div>Being able to claim descent from some noble is very different from saying I am claiming to be a titled noble like a baron or count or duke or king in my own right which is the point of the actual OP. His PCs want to be able to become titled nobles. Not to say they are some long forgotten descendant from some noble NPC. Being some long lost cousin of a duke isn't the same as being the duke.<br /> <br /> There's no problem pointing out some exceptions in my post - hey, sometimes people were able to forge documents that said they were part of the noble class by being some forgotten line of descent from a noble. But that is not the same thing as people making themselves a duke. When such people are able to seize real power (and forge documents establishing noble birth), they tend to be in exceptional circumstances.<br /> <br /> Forgeries are only able to work in certain circumstances. Either its a time when more advanced techniques don't exist to detect them (the Donation of Constantine was accepted for many centuries before Lorenzo Valla proved it was bullshit), or when mere sheer brutal military keeps people from raising objections. Let's not pretend that's normal. And how relevant that is to Changeling is very debatable given who becomes a Changeling in what human families. Has any Changeling become noble because of forged documents? I suppose it may be possible, but let's not pretend that is a common way to do so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-dreaming/1449274-attaining-titles-in-concordia?p=1449426#post1449426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah most peasants didn't become Emperors... because what they did was stay at home and farm. Most Nobles didn't become Emperors. Jumping up the ranks is all about being the exception.. but.. the Dreaming values narrative more than base reality...</div> </div> </div> </div>I am glad you agree with me, because my posts also explicitly pointed out that myth works at a different level and therefore the Dreaming allows things that history does not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}